Interview 1 (Darren – Iteration 3)

Interviewer: So, after you use this product, I have some questions. What was your first impression when you opened the YouKu Club platform?
Interviewee: I think it's a good question. To me, my first impression was that it looks clean and simple. The layout is easy to understand, so I can see popular clubs right away on the home screen. It feels like a mobile app designed for students.

Interviewer: How easy was it to find a club that matched your interest?
Interviewee: I think it was quite easy. The platform shows recommended clubs and categories like sports, culture, and faculty. For example, I like technology, so I quickly noticed the UQ Computing Society. The member numbers also help me see which clubs are active.

Interviewer: Did the search function and club categories help you discover relevant clubs?
Interviewee: Yes, definitely. The categories were very helpful. They group clubs by type, so I didn't need to scroll through all clubs. The search bar at the top of the club's page would also be useful if I had a specific club in mind. However, I didn't get to test the search function because the screenshots don't show search results.

Interviewer: Were there any parts of the interface that you found confusing or difficult to use?
Interviewee: Not really, but I noticed a small issue on the Clubs page. The UQ Board Games Club and UQ Basketball Club both have the abbreviated version “UB”. This would be confusing if two clubs share the same code. Also, the profile page shows I have joined zero clubs, but there is no clear join button on the club detail page in the screenshot.

Interviewer: Did the club detail page provide enough information for you to decide whether to join a club?
Interviewee: Yes, definitely. For the Malaysian Students Association, the detail page gives useful information like what the club does, weekly activities, meeting location, and email contact. This helped me understand the club's culture and time commitment. I would feel confident joining after reading that page.

Interviewer: How easy was it to navigate between different pages and features on this website?
Interviewee: To me, I think it's very easy. The bottom navigation bar has Home, Clubs, and Profile. You can tap between them without getting lost. Each club has a “View More” button that leads to the detailed page, which is really a clear and expected design.

Interviewer: What information or features do you think are missing from the platform?
Interviewee: I think the first is a join button on the club detail page. Upcoming event dates or a calendar is also important. There should also be a way to contact club leaders directly from the app. The profile page has a section for joined clubs, which currently shows zero, but there is no space to display clubs after joining. Lastly, there is no notification section and no notification alerts.

Interviewer: How useful do you think this website would be for new UQ students looking to get involved on campus?
Interviewee: I think it's very useful because new students often don't know where to start. This platform puts all clubs in one place. It shows categories, member counts, and active information. It saves time compared to searching on different websites or social media pages.

Interviewer: Compared to other ways of finding clubs, such as social media or asking friends, what's your impression of this website?
Interviewee: It performs better for finding clubs quickly because it is designed for mobile and has a clean interface. Social media like Facebook or Instagram is good for seeing event photos, but it's harder to find all clubs in one place. Asking friends is helpful but limited. This platform combines the best parts: organized lists and detailed information.

Interviewer: If this platform were officially available, how likely would you be to use it in the future and why?
Interviewee: I would be very likely to use it because it is simple, fast, and focuses exactly on what I need, like finding and learning about clubs. If they add a join button and show joined clubs on the profile page, it would be even better. As a student with an IELTS of 6.5 English level, I found all the text easy to read and understand.
